<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Unique Facts About My Area – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting Our Country</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Unique Facts About My Area</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present personal opinions on a variety of topics. Interactive: Engage in problem-solving discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can express personal opinions about places to visit by interpreting suggestions and explaining implied meanings. (e.g., "I think when someone says, 'It's popular with tourists,' they really mean it might be overcrowded or too commercial.").
Can solve problems related to directions and locations in the city (e.g., "To get to the museum, go straight down this street, take the second left, and it will be on your right.").
Can participate in a discussion about planning activities for a friend's visit by asking and answering questions to build a detailed itinerary (e.g., "What time does the museum open?" "After the museum, we can visit the seafood market. What do you think?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of famous Panamanian attractions (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Canal, Biomuseo, local beaches like Santa Catalina, a local seafood market). Students work in pairs to name the places and share one unique fact or personal opinion about each. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What's unique about this area?" or "What do you think of this location?".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing opinions and making recommendations using target grammar and vocabulary. For example, "I think Casco Viejo is a breathtaking landmark because it showcases a lot of our history. It was once a neglected area, but after its restoration, it has become a popular destination for tourists." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of words like "interesting" (/ˈɪn.trə.stɪŋ/) and "restaurant" (/ˈres.trɒnt/) during modeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I asking you to state a fact, or to give your personal feeling about a place?" (Expected answer: "Your personal feeling/opinion.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'must-see attraction,' what do I mean?" (Expected answer: "It's a place that is highly recommended to visit.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting Our Country")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays an audio recording of a dialogue (or presents a short written dialogue) between a local Panamanian (Maria) and her friend (Tom), who is visiting from abroad. In the dialogue, Tom asks for recommendations on local attractions and how to get around, while Maria offers suggestions, gives directions, and shares unique facts. The dialogue naturally integrates the target grammar (Past simple and present perfect, Future with "will" and "going to," Reported speech) and vocabulary (visit, area, location, attraction, plan, direction, market, museum, restaurant, beach, landmark, guide, itinerary, go straight, turn right, next to, recommend, breathtaking, tranquil, interesting, unique, must-see).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participate in group planning for events or trips, offering directions and suggestions clearly, ensuring everyone understands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen/read for gist: "What are Maria and Tom discussing?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False statements: Students decide if statements about the dialogue are true or false (e.g., "Tom has visited the Biomuseo before." "Maria recommends the seafood market for lunch.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information matching: Match recommended places with a reason or unique fact mentioned in the dialogue (e.g., Casco Viejo → its history, restoration, and unique architecture; the seafood market → the best local seafood).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using Past simple and present perfect for contrasting actions ("Casco Viejo was once a neglected area, but it has become..."), Future with "will" and "going to" for intentions ("I will book a tour," "we're going to try..."), and Reported speech ("They said that the best seafood is found..."). Vocabulary includes nouns (area, location, attraction, market, museum, restaurant, landmark, itinerary, guide), verbs (see, visit, plan, explore, recommend, direct, advise), adjectives (breathtaking, tranquil, unique, must-see, local, famous, safe), and prepositions for directions (go straight, turn right, turn left, across from, next to, between). Pronunciation practice focuses on contrastive sounds (/st/ in "stay," /ks/ in "explain") and reduced syllables ("interesting," "restaurant").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Transformation):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or a worksheet. Students complete them orally or in pairs, focusing on the target grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Panama City ____ (be) a small town, but now it ____ (become) a bustling city." (Past simple/Present perfect)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I ____ (book) a table for us at the seafood ____ (market) tonight." (Future with 'will' and vocabulary)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Our guide ____ (say) that the museum ____ (showcase) our cultural heritage." (Reported speech and vocabulary)</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students orally share their completed sentences, and the teacher corrects pronunciation and grammar.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Substitution Drill & Role Card Prompts):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides a basic dialogue frame for asking/giving directions. Students substitute locations and direction phrases.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Excuse me, how do I get to the [museum]?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Go straight for two [blocks], then turn [left] at the traffic light. It will be [across from] the [cathedral]."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher provides flashcards with different locations (e.g., "local restaurant," "historical landmark," "tranquil beach") and prepositions (e.g., "next to," "between," "behind"). Students swap cards and repeat the dialogue, practicing pronunciation of contrastive sounds like /st/ in "stay" and /tʃ/ in "chance" when they mention "chance to see."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Mini-Itinerary Planning):</b> Students receive a simple map of a fictional Panamanian town with 3-4 key attractions (e.g., "Art Museum," "Central Market," "Botanical Gardens"). In pairs, they pretend one is a local and the other is a tourist. They plan a short itinerary for 2-3 hours, asking for and giving directions to each location and making recommendations. The teacher reminds them to use phrases like "I recommend," "What do you think of...?", and to explain implied meanings if needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting Our Country")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-Play and Itinerary Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in groups of three. One student is a visiting friend, and the other two are local hosts. Their task is to collaboratively plan a detailed 2-day itinerary for the visiting friend, focusing on "Unique Facts About My Area" and considering the scenario "Visiting Our Country."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The local hosts must suggest at least four attractions, explaining why they are "must-see" or "unique," using opinions and contrasting past with present where applicable (e.g., "This beach was always tranquil, but recently it has become more popular.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must give clear directions between at least two locations using appropriate prepositions (e.g., "To get to the museum, go straight down this street, take the second left, and it will be on your right.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The visiting friend must actively ask questions, express preferences, and interpret suggestions (e.g., "I think when someone says, 'It's popular with tourists,' they really mean it might be overcrowded or too commercial.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups should use future with "will" and "going to" for their plans (e.g., "We will visit the Panama Canal in the morning," "We're going to try that new restaurant"). They should also incorporate reported speech (e.g., "They said the best time to see the ships is early morning").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group presents their 2-day itinerary orally to another group or the class, taking turns to explain their decisions and directions. The teacher reminds students to pay attention to clear pronunciation, especially of words like "showcase" and "explore."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' performance in the collaborative role-play and itinerary presentation from Stage 4. Peers provide feedback using a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, especially target vocabulary and pronunciation points.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and varied use of nouns, verbs, adjectives, and prepositions related to the theme.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Correct application of Past simple/present perfect, Future with "will"/"going to," and Reported speech.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of suggestions and directions:</b> Ability to express opinions, make recommendations, and give clear, logical directions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and interaction:</b> Active participation in problem-solving discussions and engaging effectively with group members.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new phrase or way to give directions I learned today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easiest/most challenging to (express opinions / give directions / use reported speech) because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What unique fact about my area did I enjoy talking about the most, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now about giving directions to a visitor in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., student engagement during role-play, clear use of target vocabulary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with pronunciation or grammatical structures (e.g., reported speech) for next lesson? (e.g., more direct pronunciation drills, sentence frames for complex structures).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition):</b> In the next lesson, students will take their planned itineraries and write a descriptive email or travel blog post, consolidating the vocabulary and grammar used today into a written product. They will focus on constructing clear paragraphs that describe places, give directions, and incorporate personal opinions about unique facts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>